  "query_preview": "..Satellite's total precipitable water shows a \nnorthwest oriented finger of values up to 0.9 inches, near \nHermosillo, and another of 0.6-0.65 inches oriented east- west, \nlocated just northeast of the Gulf of California. The Gulf's \nmoisture is being pushed in to Southeast Arizona from the low \npressure system over the Pacific Ocean. This moisture push will \ncontinue this morning and into today's weather story. \n\nPrecipitable water content is forecasted to reach up to nearly\none inch in Tucson per the latest HREF run. A sign for a more\nactive Sunday starting this afternoon. Thunderstorm and rain\nshower chances will be 10-30%, starting with areas from Tucson\nand south; then shifting coverage and expanding chances up to\nnearly 50%-90% in Cochise County and north towards the White \nMountains by this evening. The low pressure system will be moving\neast throughout the day, shifting the favorability of storms as\nwell as pushing the moisture plume. With the drier low levels \nand DCAPE of 1000-1300 J/kg, there will be 10-30% chances for \ngusty winds exceeding 35 mph. Some locales can experience gusts \nup to 45 mph, a less than 5% chance of occuring. This will \nraise blowing dust concerns during the late afternoon and early \nevening. \n\nRainfall amounts are generally less than a tenth of an inch, and\nleaning to more virga showers and dry lightning threat. Yet, \nthere is potential for isolated storms to produce more rainfall \nof 0.25-0.5 inches due to the higher precipitable water content\nto work with. Currently, there is a 25% chance for greater than\n0.25 inches south of Tucson and pockets east and northeast of \nTucson during the evening and overnight hours. \n\nChances for rain showers and thunderstorms will continue on\nMemorial Day, mainly east and northeast of Tucson. Activity will\nstart to diminish in the late afternoon. Chances will linger on\nearly Tuesday. As the low continue moves into the region, the\npressure gradient will increase during the holiday. This will \nlead to southwest breezes of 15-20 mph with gusts up to 30 mph \non Monday and on Tuesday. \n\nEnsemble models are still on track for the next weather system\nthat will be moving into the Great Basin, right behind today\nthrough Tuesday's weather system. This midweek system will bring\ndry and widespread breezy conditions on Wednesday and Thursday.",
